  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=-15,$ $b=k,$ and $c=-60,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(k)^{2}-4(-15)(-60) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-3\\,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3600 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =3600 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{3600} \\\\ k & =\\pm 60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\pm 60.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
